== Problem description ==
Front proximity and ambient light sensor failure after water damage or improper flex replacement. Replacing the flex with a non-programmed or aftermarket part removes auto-brightness and proximity functionality.

== Symptoms ==

* Auto-brightness toggle missing in Settings.
* Screen does not turn off during calls.
* History of water damage or prior sensor replacement.

== Diagnostic Steps ==

# Confirm device history (liquid damage, part replacement).
# Test proximity function during calls.
# Check Settings > Accessibility > Display & Text size > auto-brightness toggle.
# Inspect flex under microscope for corrosion or damage.
# Confirm issue is not caused by display/logic board.

[[File:Photosensitive-sensor.png|thumb|JCID Photosensitive Sensor Flex]]

== Repair Steps ==

==== 1. Prepare Tools & Firmware ====

* Download the correct IPSW firmware for your iPhone model from [https://ipsw.me ipsw.me] or [https://www.3u.com 3uTools].
* Install JCID Repair Assistant and 3uTools on your computer.
* Have a JCID V1SE/V1S Pro programmer and JCID photosensitive board ready.

----

==== 2. Perform Brushing & Unbind ====
This step unbinds factory data, preparing the device to pair  a new JCID sensor.

# Connect the iPhone to your computer and open JCID Repair Assistant.
# Click Read Phone to detect the device.[[File:Read phone.png|center|thumb|Read phone in JCID Repair Assistant]]
# Select Brushing mode.[[File:Jcid brushing option.png|thumb|JCID Brushing Unbind|center]]
# Tick both options under Brushing Options to enable unbinding.[[File:Tick.png|border|center|thumb|Tick these 2 options and select flashing and unbind]]
# Click Start Flashing & Unbinding and select your downloaded IPSW firmware.[[File:Select-ipsw.png|center|thumb|Select downloaded ipsw]]
# Let JCID complete the brushing/unbind process.[[File:Brushing-complete.png|center|thumb|Brushing unbind complete]]
# Once done, the iPhone will be in Recovery Mode (expected).
# Open 3uTools, flash the same firmware using a  Flash.

----

==== 3. Program the New JCID Sensor ====

# Connect your JCID V1SE or V1S Pro programmer to your PC.
# Attach the JCID photosensitive board and the new JCID sensor.
# In JCID software, click 'Start testing' to verify the new sensor is functional.[[File:Start-testing-fpc.png|center|thumb|Start Testing FPC]]
# Click 'Activate FPC' to pair the new JCID sensor with the iPhone.[[File:Activate-fpc.png|center|thumb|Activate FPC]]
# Once activation is completed, ECID should match in both columns.[[File:Matching ecid.png|center|thumb|ECID Matching ]]

----

==== 4. Install & Test ====

# Install the newly programmed sensor back into the iPhone.
# Reassemble the device carefully.
# Boot up the iPhone and test proximity sensor and auto-brightness functionality.